      \field{abstract}{Two mixtures of normal distributions, created by persistent changes in volatility, are compared as models for asset returns. A Markov chain with two states for volatility is contrasted with an autoregressive Gaussian process for the logarithm of volatility. The conditional variances of asset returns are shown to have a bimodal distribution for the former process when volatility is persistent that contrasts with a unimodal distribution for the latter process. A test procedure based upon this contrast shows that a lognormal distribution for sterling/dollar volatility is far more credible than only two volatility states.}
